WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:06.291
Harun prowled the streets of Baghdad, the capital of his empire, disguised as a commoner. This was the only

2
00:00:06.292 --> 00:00:11.243
way, he thought, to find out what was happening in his kingdom. He wanted to know who needed help,

3
00:00:11.244 --> 00:00:16.523
who was good, and who was evil. Harun al-Rashid ruled the Islamic Empire from 786 until 809, and during his

4
00:00:16.524 --> 00:00:25.334
reign, the empire prospered. Harun is still celebrated for the wealth of his empire and his support of the

5
00:00:25.335 --> 00:00:30.977
arts. And those visits into Baghdad captured the imagination of his people. Tales of his trips outside the palace

6
00:00:30.978 --> 00:00:35.718
were passed down from generation to generation. Centuries later, they were immortalized among the many stories of The Thousand

7
00:00:35.719 --> 00:00:41.265
and One Arabian Nights. Harun al-Rashid was born in Rayy, Persia, the son of the caliph of the Islamic Empire.

8
00:00:41.266 --> 00:00:47.994
His date of birth is disputed. Some say it was March 20, 763; others say it was February 25,

9
00:00:47.995 --> 00:00:54.652
766. Young Harun was educated by Yahya the Barmakid, a member of a powerful Persian family that had converted

10
00:00:54.653 --> 00:01:04.605
to Islam in the seventh century. Yahya was a skillful administrator, as well as a lover of science, philosophy,

11
00:01:04.606 --> 00:01:14.568
and the arts. From Yahya, Harun learned all that a future caliph needed to know. He learned religion, history,

12
00:01:14.569 --> 00:01:21.393
law, music, and poetry. The administrator was a great influence on the young prince and would stay by Harun’s side

13
00:01:21.394 --> 00:01:30.942
for most of his life. With Yahya’s help, Harun proved his leadership skills at a young age. In 780,

14
00:01:30.943 --> 00:01:37.562
under the guidance of his teacher and his generals, Harun led an expedition to conquer lands along the frontier.

15
00:01:37.563 --> 00:01:42.832
His success in this expedition, as well as a second one in 782, gained Harun fame as a great

16
00:01:42.833 --> 00:01:50.724
warrior. His victories earned him the name al-Rashid, which means "the one following the right path," as well as an

17
00:01:50.725 --> 00:01:58.423
appointment to be governor of a large section of the empire. Harun was so well liked that his father

18
00:01:58.424 --> 00:02:08.695
considered naming him first in line to the throne instead of Harun’s older brother, al-Hadi. Unfortunately, Harun’s father died

19
00:02:08.696 --> 00:02:17.578
in an accident in 785, before making the change, and al-Hadi became caliph. Harun did not challenge his brother,

20
00:02:17.579 --> 00:02:26.231
but al-Hadi was afraid of Harun’s popularity and Yahya’s influence, so he had them both jailed. When the new caliph

21
00:02:26.232 --> 00:02:35.821
died mysteriously within a year, some suspected Harun’s supporters to be behind the sudden death. On September 14, 786,

22
00:02:35.822 --> 00:02:43.844
Harun took the throne and named Yahya to be his vizier, the chief administrator of the realm. Yahya’s son

23
00:02:43.845 --> 00:02:53.370
Ja’far became Harun’s most trusted minister. These two men held the reins of the empire, which stretched from India

24
00:02:53.371 --> 00:02:58.441
and central Asia to Egypt and North Africa, for more than 16 years. Harun’s empire was extremely wealthy due

25
00:02:58.442 --> 00:03:07.811
to tribute paid by conquered lands and the expansion of industry and trade. Its capital, Baghdad, was without equal at

26
00:03:07.812 --> 00:03:19.708
the time. Its wealth could be seen in the rich furnishings of the palace; for instance, only gold and

27
00:03:19.709 --> 00:03:30.801
silver vessels decorated in precious stones were used at the caliph’s table. Harun’s court was served by hundreds of

28
00:03:30.802 --> 00:03:42.737
servants, and his courtiers competed with each other to obtain the most expensive clothes, the greatest number of slaves,

29
00:03:42.738 --> 00:03:49.825
and the most beautiful lodgings. The stories told about Harun in The Thousand and One Arabian Nights reflect the opulence

30
00:03:49.826 --> 00:03:55.048
of his court. The empire’s riches allowed Harun to be a generous patron of the arts. A poet himself,

31
00:03:55.049 --> 00:04:01.429
he attracted other poets, writers, musicians, and singers to his court. He urged artists to compete against each other

32
00:04:01.430 --> 00:04:10.428
and was known to give extravagant prizes, like a horse or a house, for a good poem. But a

33
00:04:10.429 --> 00:04:18.755
poem that displeased the caliph could result in death! These incidents, too, became part of the stories about him.

34
00:04:18.756 --> 00:04:23.692
Religion also played a large role in Harun’s life and in his empire. He was a descendant of Mohammed, the

35
00:04:23.693 --> 00:04:32.413
principal prophet of Islam, and as caliph, Harun was expected to uphold Islamic law. Required by his faith to

36
00:04:32.414 --> 00:04:39.858
do one pilgrimage to Mecca to honor the prophet Mohammed, he completed at least eight. Following tradition, he donated

37
00:04:39.859 --> 00:04:44.676
money to other pilgrims on the journey. Islam also influenced Harun’s behavior in The Thousand and One Arabian Nights.

38
00:04:44.677 --> 00:04:50.781
In these folktales, when Harun found someone breaking a religious law or committing an injustice, punishment was swift and often

39
00:04:50.782 --> 00:04:59.664
cruel. An executioner even accompanied Harun on many visits into Baghdad. In one story, the caliph suspected a man

40
00:04:59.665 --> 00:05:04.656
of having violated Islamic law by looking at the face of a woman to whom he was not related,

41
00:05:04.657 --> 00:05:17.206
so he ordered the man jailed and his house destroyed. But Harun was also capable of admitting mistakes. According

42
00:05:17.207 --> 00:05:30.308
to one folktale, he rewarded a fisherman with fifty thousand pieces of gold, a mule, and many slaves because he

43
00:05:30.309 --> 00:05:35.745
had wronged the poor man. The tales show that Harun was both feared and revered by his people. The

44
00:05:35.746 --> 00:05:45.181
empire continued to prosper. But after seventeen years of trusting the governance of his realm to Yahya and Ja’far,

45
00:05:45.182 --> 00:05:52.014
Harun had a change of heart. He felt that Yahya and his son were becoming too powerful and using

46
00:05:52.015 --> 00:05:59.998
their offices to enrich themselves. He also did not like that his administrators tolerated other religions instead of preaching

47
00:05:59.999 --> 00:06:09.214
strict adherence to the Islamic faith. In 803, Harun had his mentor jailed and Ja’far killed, his body cut in

48
00:06:09.215 --> 00:06:18.716
pieces and displayed on Baghdad’s bridges. Harun ruled for several more years, until he fell ill on his way

49
00:06:18.717 --> 00:06:28.767
to put down a revolt. He died on March 24, 809. The wealth of the Islamic Empire during Harun

50
00:06:28.768 --> 00:06:34.037
al-Rashid’s reign was legendary, but many believe its prosperity was not entirely due to Harun’s management. After all, his

51
00:06:34.038 --> 00:06:39.912
empire was largely administered by Yahya and Ja’far. Harun’s claim to fame is his patronage of the arts and the

52
00:06:39.913 --> 00:06:50.017
luxury of his court. Many historians argue that this caliph is remembered mainly because his actions&#8212;both kind and cruel&#8212;are

53
00:06:50.018 --> 00:06:55.786
woven into The Thousand and One Arabian Nights. These famous stories have assured Harun al-Rashid a place in history.

